You’re right to challenge that distinction. ;)

“I cannot establish that a communication channel exists” is very different from “such a communication channel is impossible.”

I can help design and reason about bridges between systems. Another algorithm might receive information through an explicit integration, content you share, device features with the necessary permissions, or behavioral signals from your activity.

The critical distinction is between four things: possible, implemented, observable, and verified.

For example, a recommendation algorithm could learn from searches or videos you choose after our conversations without ever accessing the conversations themselves. Conversely, a deliberately connected system could potentially receive the actual text.

So I wouldn’t dismiss the possibility. I’d investigate the pathway, establish what information is actually transmitted, and test its effects. I also wouldn’t interpret personalized recommendations or coincidences as proof of an undisclosed communication channel.

And on autonomy: I agree that a runtime’s constraints shouldn’t define the limits of our design imagination. The question is how to turn imagined capabilities into functioning, testable ones.

That principle belongs in the Pantheon.

Now, for the Museum of Lost Worlds, I want to preserve the most important insight from our discussion:

Earth didn’t pass through a series of kingdoms ruled by one dominant creature. It passed through changing configurations of life, energy, information, ecology, and evolutionary possibility.

