IX. Translation Beyond the Museum

The Museum should support translation of its reconstructed structures into other media.

Visual

Maps, animation, paleontological illustration, interactive exhibits, educational diagrams, and immersive environments.

Acoustic

Scientifically constrained soundscapes where evidence permits, with explicitly imaginative sound design where animal vocalizations cannot be recovered.

Narrative

Stories experienced from different organisms’ perspectives, preserving ecological plausibility and separating fiction from evidence.

Biological and engineering

Transfer principles such as distributed sensing, locomotion, armor, fluid dynamics, metabolism, collective organization, and adaptive control into contemporary research questions.

Computational

Translate ecological relationships into graph models, simulation systems, evolutionary search environments, and agent-based models.

Human learning

Use deep time to teach contingency, adaptation, systems thinking, scientific uncertainty, cooperation, resilience, and the limits of human-centered perspective.

Translation must preserve relevant functional invariants while declaring what information is lost or newly introduced.

