XI. Initial Build and Acceptance Tests

Stage 1 — Semantic seed

Preserve this single document as the canonical architecture until dedicated data files are justified.

It must be possible for a new AI instance to recover the purpose, evidence rules, main operators, initial exhibits, and development roadmap without access to the original conversation.

Stage 2 — Evidence-backed world records

Create records for the Cambrian, Carboniferous, Permian, Jurassic, Paleogene, and Pleistocene.

Begin with representative examples, not an exhaustive species catalog.

Stage 3 — Interactive timeline

Implement navigation by time, geography, organism, and lens.

Every displayed organism must have a plausible temporal and geographic placement.

Stage 4 — Lens transformation

Implement size, ancestry, ecology, and energy lenses.

Changing lenses must alter relationships and visual emphasis rather than merely recolor the scene.

Stage 5 — Reconstructions

Introduce optional photographs of fossils, museum scans, paleoart, 3D reconstructions, and immersive scenes.

Clearly distinguish original fossils, scans, scientific reconstructions, and generative illustrations.

Stage 6 — Counterfactual laboratory

Enable constrained alternative-evolution experiments and comparative morphology analysis.

Every hypothetical outcome must remain clearly identifiable as speculation.

Acceptance tests

* Temporal integrity: A species cannot be presented as living in a period outside its supported range without a warning.
* Geographic integrity: Species sharing an era are not automatically shown sharing a habitat.
* Evidence transparency: Visitors can trace major claims back to their sources.
* Lens independence: Different metrics can yield different biologically important organisms.
* Reconstruction honesty: Uncertain appearance and behavior remain explicitly uncertain.
* Cross-domain transfer: Functional similarities do not automatically imply shared ancestry.
* Rehydration: Another AI can reconstruct the module from this seed.
* Reversibility: The original evidence remains accessible after any visual or narrative transformation.
* Delta tracking: Model updates preserve what changed, why, and how confidence was affected.
* Extensibility: New discoveries and alternative taxonomies can be incorporated without losing prior records.

